WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.791
This is Anthropic, Read Aloud. Today: What work can robots do?.

2
00:00:07.091 --> 00:00:08.559
What work can robots do?

3
00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:21.470
What work can robots do? Anthropic's robot exposure index finds robots can do 3/4 of U S physical job tasks, but are cost-competitive for only 0.3 percent.

4
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.348
From Anthropic Research, published September 30, 2026.

5
00:00:27.348 --> 00:00:28.320
Key findings.

6
00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:34.758
We present a robot exposure index based on how well robots can perform job tasks today.

7
00:00:34.998 --> 00:00:53.828
Robots, which we define as autonomous physical machines that sense and act, can perform three-quarters of physical tasks in the U S, making up 34 percent of working hours, but mostly in limited settings. Workers exposed to robots are more likely to be male, less educated, and lower paid.

8
00:00:53.948 --> 00:01:07.724
For example, driving and warehouse jobs are highly exposed to currently available robots; nursing and general repair jobs are not, since present-day robots can do little of their work even in highly controlled environments.

9
00:01:07.964 --> 00:01:25.728
Overall, about 80 percent of job tasks by working time are exposed to either robots or L L Ms. Robots do work where L L Ms cannot. The remaining unexposed work is highly interpersonal or requires physical skills that robots today don’t have.

10
00:01:25.968 --> 00:01:43.145
While robots can do most physical work tasks today, they are much more expensive than human labor. Robots are cost-competitive for just 0.3 percent of job tasks. If robot price declines follow past trends, it will take 40 years for that share to reach 10 percent.

11
00:01:43.265 --> 00:01:50.857
Beyond price, factors including capabilities, preferences, and regulations pose further barriers to robot automation.

12
00:01:51.097 --> 00:02:08.005
Over the past 50 years, jobs more exposed to robots experienced greater declines in wages and employment than others. At the same time, job exposure has grown: each year, robots have become able to do about 2 percent of the physical work they previously couldn’t.

13
00:02:08.805 --> 00:02:09.649
Introduction.

14
00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:22.887
Advances in large language models have raised the possibility of automating large swaths of work. But many jobs are physical. A I’s impact on the economy will in part depend on robotics.¹

15
00:02:23.337 --> 00:02:45.780
Predicting the pace of robot advances is difficult, but enumerating capabilities today, we argue, can give insight into the coming years. We develop a measure of job exposure to robots, using Claude to assess how well present-day robots can perform work tasks. A job is more exposed when robots can do more of its tasks in less controlled environments.

16
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:51.175
A robot is cost-competitive when it can do the same task for cheaper than a human worker.

17
00:02:51.625 --> 00:03:04.895
We find that robots can already perform 74 percent of physical tasks in the U S, making up 34 percent of working hours. Robots and L L Ms together expose all but one-fifth of employment.

18
00:03:05.345 --> 00:03:28.619
But we also find significant barriers to adoption: most robots require highly structured environments, and are cost-competitive with people for just 0.3 percent of work. If robot price declines follow past trends, it will take 40 years for that share to reach just 10 percent. After cost, the main barrier is capability, such as the dexterity needed to untangle wires.

19
00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:34.819
Human preferences and regulations further limit robot adoption for a significant share of tasks.

20
00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:54.679
Our core premise is that jobs are more likely to be impacted when robots can already do their work today. A backtest across 50 years validates this approach: from 1977 to today, jobs that were more exposed to existing robots experienced wage and employment declines in later decades.

21
00:03:55.129 --> 00:04:07.593
If the past is any guide, taxi drivers and warehouse packers will see changes sooner than nurses and mechanics. We expect that physical work will first be automated where robots have a foothold today.

22
00:04:08.393 --> 00:04:10.009
Robots today and tomorrow.

23
00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:22.000
Most robots today operate in controlled environments like factories. Robots usually need to be programmed to interact with the physical world, whereas humans can adapt to their work environments.

24
00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:35.912
This has been a major hurdle for commercially viable robots.² But A I helps robots interpret and respond to their surroundings, allowing warehouse robots and autonomous vehicles to operate alongside humans.³

25
00:04:36.362 --> 00:04:59.657
Many observers expect A I to improve robot capabilities quickly.⁴ Although spending on robots remains about 1 percent of total U S equipment investment, business surveys suggest U S robot adoption could nearly double within three years.⁵ And firms are investing billions to develop A I-powered robots that match human physical abilities.⁶

26
00:05:00.107 --> 00:05:20.821
It’s difficult to predict exactly how these efforts will affect jobs and productivity. A ranking of occupations by current robot task coverage suggests where the impacts will appear first. Robots should affect jobs that they can already do before jobs they could do only with new technology and, in some cases, accommodating regulations.

27
00:05:20.941 --> 00:05:41.768
Current capabilities are also concrete and measurable, while forecasts of future capabilities must bet on which technologies will succeed.⁷ Robots still in development support our focus on current exposure: firms are testing humanoids in car factories and warehouses, structured environments where robots are already common.⁸

28
00:05:42.568 --> 00:05:43.802
Measuring exposure.

29
00:05:44.252 --> 00:05:59.631
Our analyses use data from ONET, a database of around 900 occupations linked with descriptions of around 19000 job tasks. We identify a set of physical job tasks that we think could not be automated without robots.

30
00:05:59.751 --> 00:06:16.301
To do so, we have Claude score task descriptions on a rubric measuring physical, cognitive, and interpersonal work requirements.⁹ The task “Dig trenches” is physical; so is “Teach dance students,” though this also requires cognitive and interpersonal skills.

31
00:06:16.421 --> 00:06:31.013
While typists use their hands to “Compute and verify totals on report forms, requisitions, or bills, using adding machine or calculator,” this task doesn’t count. Appendix A gives details, and Appendix F lists our prompts.

32
00:06:31.463 --> 00:06:39.310
Our task-level measure of robot exposure asks: can a robot today perform this task, and if so under what circumstances?

33
00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:58.743
We take robots to mean autonomous physical machines that sense and act, which includes car washes that scan cars to adjust sprayers and excludes teleoperated surgical machines fully controlled by a surgeon.¹⁰ We measure degrees of exposure by the kind of work environment a robot needs to perform a task.¹¹

34
00:06:59.193 --> 00:07:05.636
Specifically, we sort tasks into four tiers of increasing exposure by where a robot can perform each task:

35
00:07:06.086 --> 00:07:08.640
E0: Robot cannot perform task.

36
00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:15.597
E1: Robot can perform task in a purpose-built robotic work environment, like a factory assembly line.

37
00:07:15.837 --> 00:07:21.653
E2: Robot can perform task in a structured human work facility, like a logistics warehouse.

38
00:07:21.893 --> 00:07:26.921
E3: Robot can perform task in an unstructured environment, like a city road.

39
00:07:27.371 --> 00:07:29.765
Figure 1 also displays this rubric.

40
00:07:30.215 --> 00:07:48.199
Figure 1: Figure 1: Task robot exposure rubric Job tasks identified as physical are rated on this robot exposure rubric. For example, the job task of unloading boxes from a truck at a warehouse is rated E2 if there’s a warehouse robot that can do that task.

41
00:07:48.649 --> 00:08:04.862
We think environmental control is a good measure of near-term automation risk. Because it’s hard for robots to adapt to unpredictable environments, most deployed robots work in engineered environments, like those that spray paint cars on assembly lines.

42
00:08:04.982 --> 00:08:13.221
While these difficulties are thought to have slowed physical automation in the past, A I-powered robots could better adapt to their environments.¹²

43
00:08:13.671 --> 00:08:32.145
To determine exposure, we instruct Claude to search for specific robots relevant to each task and assess their capabilities and operating environments, quoting sources directly. We ask whether a robot could perform a task in versions of that task’s typical work environment that are more or less structured.

44
00:08:32.265 --> 00:08:49.870
Getting robots to do seemingly simple tasks like loading a dishwasher requires many complex physical skills, so only demonstrated robot capabilities count.¹³ Robots must also do a task similarly well to humans, factoring in reliability, error rates, and speed.

45
00:08:50.320 --> 00:09:00.771
For instance, self-driving cars couldn’t “Drive taxicabs or privately owned vehicles to transport passengers” on real roads in the early 2010s.

46
00:09:00.891 --> 00:09:19.664
But they did drive around mock towns built for testing, a step toward today’s autonomous vehicles.¹⁴ Our rubric would rate driving passengers at exposure level E1 at that time (robot can perform task in a purpose-built robotic work environment) and E3 today (in an unstructured environment).¹⁵

47
00:09:20.114 --> 00:09:42.984
This rubric requires many judgment calls. The ONET task statements are often terse, and omit details that may be easy for humans but hard for robots.¹⁶ To describe work more concretely, we elicit detailed examples of how tasks are performed today, and how often these occur. Claude then scores exposure for these examples using web search to gauge robot capabilities.

48
00:09:43.104 --> 00:10:02.538
Cited sources must show robot deployments, commercial sales, or demonstrations, and results are similar if we omit ratings relying on demonstrations. Task exposure is set by majority rule: the least structured environment in which robots can do at least half of a task’s examples, weighted by time.¹⁷

49
00:10:02.988 --> 00:10:11.483
Consider the task “Dig trenches.” We first ask Claude to provide examples describing how workers perform this task today and how often.

50
00:10:11.603 --> 00:10:31.610
For example, Claude estimates that 25 percent of the time, this task requires “cutting a linear trench in open ground.” Another 20 percent of the time, it requires “careful digging by hand around existing buried pipes, flowlines, cables and conduit.” These activities require different sorts of physical abilities,

51
00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:39.538
like navigation and strength (linear trenches) compared to fine motor dexterity and perception (digging by hand around pipes).

52
00:10:39.988 --> 00:11:03.732
Claude rates the first example at exposure E3, citing a control system that retrofits hydraulic excavators to autonomously dig trenches.¹⁸ Robots today can’t carefully dig around buried pipes, so the latter example is unexposed at E0. More examples for “Dig trenches” resemble careful digging than linear trenching, so overall this task is unexposed in our ratings.

53
00:11:04.182 --> 00:11:14.657
Figure 2 gives examples of task ratings, along with fuller descriptions of each exposure tier from our exposure prompt and a robot cited to support each rating.

54
00:11:14.777 --> 00:11:27.612
We also calculate the share of physical tasks in each tier: here and throughout, each task is weighted by the number of workers who do it, and by the fraction of working time they spend on it, which we estimate with Claude.¹⁹

55
00:11:28.062 --> 00:11:54.849
Figure 2: Figure 2: Example task exposure ratings Levels of robot exposure are as described in the prompt we use to rate exposure with Claude. Exposure is rated for 7594 job tasks classified as physical. Physical task shares in column 2 weight tasks by the fraction of time spent on each task in an occupation (estimated with Claude) and that occupation’s employment (BLS, 2025).

56
00:11:54.969 --> 00:12:15.632
For example, the RelayRx robot delivers drugs in hospitals. Hospitals are structured human workplaces, so the task “Deliver medications or pharmaceutical supplies to patients, nursing stations, or surgery” is rated E2. Tasks rated E2 make up 22 percent of physical tasks by working time.

57
00:12:16.082 --> 00:12:38.418
Tasks that robots can’t do (E0) make up about a quarter of physical tasks by estimated working time. These tasks require physical skills that are hard for robots, like fine motor dexterity for “Bleach, dye, or tint hair, using applicator or brush” and strength, balance, and mobility for “Erect scaffolding or ladders for assembling structures above ground level.”

58
00:12:38.868 --> 00:12:59.965
Robots can do half of physical tasks in purpose-built environments but not in wider settings (E1). Food prep robots pack ready-to-eat meals on conveyor belts, and new models with A I can adjust to different ingredients, portions, and trays, so the task “Assemble meal trays with foods in accordance with patients’ diets” is rated E1.

59
00:13:00.415 --> 00:13:21.653
Another 22 percent of physical tasks can be done by robots in structured human workplaces (E2). Hospital delivery robots, for example, “Deliver medications or pharmaceutical supplies to patients, nursing stations, or surgery.” Today’s robots do only 2 percent of physical tasks in unstructured environments (E3).

60
00:13:21.773 --> 00:13:26.915
These often involve driving, done by autonomous cars, tractors, and other vehicles.²⁰

61
00:13:27.365 --> 00:13:33.339
Our accompanying data release contains Claude’s reasoning and cited sources for all rated tasks.

62
00:13:33.459 --> 00:13:52.957
For example, Claude rates the task “Weld components in flat, vertical, or overhead positions” E1, and quotes an article about A I-powered robot welders: “Path Robotics said both of its welding cells can autonomously weld steel parts and are deployed in fabrication shops across the U S and Canada.”²¹

63
00:13:53.407 --> 00:14:10.045
We define a robot exposure index for jobs that averages a job’s task exposure ratings on a 0 to 3 scale. This index rises with the share of exposed tasks and robot capabilities on those tasks, weighting tasks by estimated working time.

64
00:14:10.495 --> 00:14:31.925
An ideal exposure index would perfectly predict robot automation in the coming years. Since we can’t look forward in time, we instead try to validate our measure using historical data on jobs and task descriptions. We rate robot exposure in several years since 1977, and link exposure to changes in wages and employment.

65
00:14:32.045 --> 00:14:49.783
When a job was more exposed to robots, its wages and employment fell in later decades, even after accounting for industry trends and other potential confounders. These results, detailed in Appendix B, give us some confidence that our measure can predict future job disruption.

66
00:14:50.583 --> 00:14:52.051
What work can robots do?

67
00:14:52.501 --> 00:15:14.647
Figure 3 summarizes robot exposure across all job tasks in the U S economy, weighting tasks by the estimated fraction of time spent per task for an occupation and that occupation’s employment. Cognitive and interpersonal work makes up 54 percent of tasks by working time. The remaining 46 percent of tasks are physical.

68
00:15:14.767 --> 00:15:40.239
As a share of all tasks, around 12 percent cannot be done by any robot today (E0), 23 percent can be done by robots in specially built environments (E1), 10 percent in structured human workplaces (E2), and 1 percent in unstructured environments (E3). Counting any exposure level, 74 percent of physical work, or 34 percent of all work, can be done by robots in some circumstances.

69
00:15:40.689 --> 00:16:04.959
Figure 3: Figure 3: Job tasks by robot exposure All job tasks are included. Tasks are weighted by the fraction of time an occupation spends on each task as well as that occupation’s employment. Robot exposure is rated for tasks identified as physical. For example, 23 percent of worker time is spent on tasks that robots can do in a purpose-built environment.

70
00:16:05.079 --> 00:16:28.810
Example tasks by category: teaching classes, designing systems (Cognitive and interpersonal); taking people into custody, providing first aid (E0); dispensing medications, assembling electronics (E1); cleaning floors, moving warehouse goods (E2); regulating field irrigation, inspecting property from the air, and driving, which robots perform in unstructured environments (E3).

71
00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:36.472
Employment data come from the BLS (2025), and we use Claude to estimate occupation time on tasks.

72
00:16:36.922 --> 00:16:46.266
Robots can do physical tasks in many parts of the economy. Autonomous mobile warehouse robots drive to loading docks and inside trailers.

73
00:16:46.386 --> 00:17:08.786
They use suction cups to grab packages and load them onto mobile conveyor belts.²² Since these robots navigate structured human workplaces, many warehousing tasks are rated E2, like “Move freight, stock, or other materials to and from storage or production areas, loading docks, delivery vehicles, ships, or containers, by hand or using trucks, tractors,

74
00:17:08.906 --> 00:17:17.735
or other equipment.” Advances in navigation and handling have made robots like these common in logistics and transportation.²³

75
00:17:18.185 --> 00:17:33.685
Figure 4 shows the 10 most exposed occupations by our robot exposure index. Of these 10 occupations, 9 are vehicle operators. Robots cited for driving jobs include autonomous cars, tractors, trucks, and pavers.

76
00:17:33.805 --> 00:17:54.178
Taxi drivers lead exposure with an index of 2.2.²⁴ Since driving is most of the job, their median task by working time is exposed at E3, while auxiliary tasks like “Vacuum and clean interiors and wash and polish exteriors of automobiles” are doable by robots but in more structured settings than open roads.

77
00:17:54.298 --> 00:18:03.766
Autonomous vehicles have yet to upend driving jobs at scale, but their capabilities suggest that these jobs are at higher risk of automation than others.²⁵

78
00:18:04.216 --> 00:18:28.749
Figure 4: Figure 4: Most exposed occupations Exposure ranges from 0 (no task can be done by a robot) to 3 (every task can be done by a robot in an unstructured environment). Job exposure averages task exposure, weighting by time spent on each task. A highly exposed task is listed for each occupation along with a cited robot that can perform this task.

79
00:18:28.869 --> 00:18:45.914
The 10 most exposed occupations with at least 20000 jobs are displayed. For example, averaging task exposure for shuttle drivers and chauffeurs gives an index of 2.0 out of 3. Claude cites Waymo autonomous vehicles as performing some of their tasks.

80
00:18:46.034 --> 00:19:00.835
Employment data from BLS (2025) sometimes combine several ONET occupation titles; we split their employment equally, as for recycling and reclamation workers. We estimate task time requirements with Claude.

81
00:19:01.285 --> 00:19:22.426
An occupation can cover many work settings. In the U S, robots may only operate in some of these settings, while robots abroad sometimes cover all of them. For example, stockers and order fillers do the task “Stock shelves, racks, cases, bins, and tables with new or transferred merchandise” in retail stores and in warehouses.

82
00:19:22.546 --> 00:19:45.144
In U S warehouses, A I-powered robots with touch sensors pick and stow merchandise.²⁶ In U S retail, mobile robots scan for empty shelves, but must alert humans to restock them.²⁷ Robots in Japanese convenience stores restock fridges themselves.²⁸ Since stocker robots require items to be brought near them, this task is rated E1.

83
00:19:45.264 --> 00:19:58.992
We estimate that stockers spend 70 percent of their time on tasks rated E1 like this one, and another 16 percent on tasks rated E2. In total, they score 1.0 on the robot exposure index.

84
00:19:59.442 --> 00:20:19.600
Jobs can be exposed to robots if many of their tasks are moderately exposed, or if some of their most time-intensive tasks are highly exposed. Compare tapers, who finish drywall and whose exposure index is 1.6, with recycling and reclamation workers, who sort recycling and whose exposure index is 1.7.

85
00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:32.892
Tasks for tapers are split between unexposed and highly exposed. Workers building interior walls use paper tape and a paste called mud to smooth over seams, joints, and screws in drywall sheets.

86
00:20:33.012 --> 00:20:56.735
Mud and tape are messy materials, so robots cannot perform the task “Press paper tape over joints to embed tape into sealing compound and to seal joints.” After the first tape coat dries, workers “Apply additional coats to fill in holes and make surfaces smooth.” This task is rated E3: an autonomous drywall robot uses A I to scan walls, spray additional coats of sealant,

87
00:20:56.855 --> 00:21:07.266
and sand walls once dry.²⁹ Overall, by work time, 41 percent of tapers’ tasks are exposed at E3, while robots cannot do another 26 percent.

88
00:21:07.716 --> 00:21:19.449
In contrast, for recycling and reclamation workers, robots can perform over three-quarters of tasks by work time at level E2. Only 8 percent of their work time is unexposed.

89
00:21:19.569 --> 00:21:38.992
For example, robots with computer vision and suction grippers can do the task “Sort materials, such as metals, glass, wood, paper or plastics, into appropriate containers for recycling.”³⁰ These robots stand in for workers picking recycling from conveyor belts, so this and other tasks are rated E2.

90
00:21:39.442 --> 00:22:03.344
Robots are highly capable at specific tasks for tapers, while for recycling workers exposure is broader, but robots are less adaptable. Messy tasks for tapers may bottleneck automation. It’s also possible that the largest recycling facilities use robots early, while smaller facilities adopt them later. Either way, robots seem likely to change both jobs if widely deployed.

91
00:22:04.144 --> 00:22:06.250
Characteristics of exposed jobs.

92
00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:12.374
Having established our exposure measure, we now consider what exposure might imply for the labor market.

93
00:22:12.824 --> 00:22:36.729
Figure 5 compares workers in the top quintile of the robot exposure index with unexposed workers, who make up another 20 percent or so of jobs. Data from the 2020 to 2024 American Community Survey show that highly exposed workers are 20 percentage points less likely to be female and 16 percentage points more likely to be Hispanic.

94
00:22:36.849 --> 00:22:48.841
These workers are also 55 percentage points less likely to hold a bachelor’s degree or higher, earn around 30 dollarsless per hour, and face an unemployment rate more than twice as high.³¹

95
00:22:49.291 --> 00:23:09.555
Occupational requirement statistics compiled by the Bureau of Labor Statistics also show that robot-exposed jobs are much more likely to require carrying weight and working in extreme heat or near hazardous contaminants. This exposure pattern is in many ways the opposite of what’s typically found for L L Ms.³²

96
00:23:10.005 --> 00:23:35.993
Figure 5: Figure 5: Occupation characteristics by robot exposure The most exposed workers are those in the top quintile of the robot exposure index; unexposed workers have zero exposure and make up about another 20 percent of jobs. Demographics, education, and labor market data come from the 2020 to 2024 American Community Survey, save for union membership and licensing.

97
00:23:36.113 --> 00:24:01.815
Union membership comes from statistics tabulated by Hirsch and others (2026) for the 2020 to 2024 Current Population Surveys. Licensing comes from the 2025 BLS Occupational Requirements Survey, as do physical demand and work environment variables (for some occupations, these come from the 2023 survey). Statistics are employment weighted.

98
00:24:02.615 --> 00:24:04.547
Robot and L L M exposure.

99
00:24:04.997 --> 00:24:21.722
We next analyze whether robots broaden job disruption risks beyond those from L L Ms alone. Drawing on Eloundou and others (2024) and Massenkoff and McCrory (2026), we compare two occupation exposure measures:

100
00:24:22.172 --> 00:24:31.275
L L M exposure: the share of job tasks for which an L L M could halve required time (called theoretical capability in our prior work ).

101
00:24:31.515 --> 00:24:44.881
L L M and robot exposure: the share of job tasks exposed to either L L Ms or robots, taking the higher of L L M exposure and E1+ robot exposure for each task.

102
00:24:45.331 --> 00:25:07.859
Robot and L L M exposure ratings measure somewhat different concepts, but both indicate that technology could do much of a given task. While integrating L L Ms and robots in actual jobs may present new challenges or enable new capabilities, these two measures provide initial insight into job exposure based on today’s capabilities.

103
00:25:08.309 --> 00:25:32.885
Figure 6 plots job exposure to L L Ms alone (in blue) and to L L Ms and robots (in orange), grouped by broad occupation category. Robots expose more and different kinds of jobs than L L Ms alone. While less than 15 percent of transportation and moving tasks are exposed to L L Ms alone, about 90 percent of these tasks are exposed with robots.

104
00:25:33.005 --> 00:25:51.863
Similarly, robots raise exposure in office and admin support jobs, which largely involve computer work and light physical tasks, to nearly 100 percent. Overall, around half of work is exposed to L L Ms alone, but that rises to 81 percent when considering robots.

105
00:25:52.313 --> 00:26:19.608
Figure 6: Figure 6: L L M and robot exposure by occupation group L L M exposure is the share of tasks rated exposed in Eloundou and others (2024) (positive 𝛽). Occupation exposure to L L Ms and robots adds tasks rated at E1 or higher robot exposure. Occupation groups are 2-digit SOC codes, and group averages weight by task time shares and employment.

106
00:26:19.728 --> 00:26:37.778
In protective service occupations, for example, 31 percent of job tasks are exposed to L L Ms, and 61 percent are exposed to L L Ms or robots. Employment data come from BLS (2025). We estimate task time shares with Claude.

107
00:26:38.228 --> 00:26:56.736
What work can’t L L Ms and robots do today? Consider personal care and service jobs, for which around 40 percent of tasks are exposed. In-person social interaction and physical contact with humans, which both L L Ms and robots struggle with, are important for these jobs.

108
00:26:56.856 --> 00:27:16.411
This example suggests that highly interpersonal work, or work requiring delicate manipulation, may be less susceptible to near-term automation.³³ Occupation groups like installation and repair, healthcare support, and community and social service fit this pattern too, with relatively low exposure.

109
00:27:16.531 --> 00:27:30.014
While L L Ms and robots can’t perform these tasks today, no occupation group overall is less than 40 percent exposed. And advances in technology may make possible new kinds of automation over time.

110
00:27:30.464 --> 00:27:49.991
Unexposed tasks also help describe what could make work hard to automate. We group unexposed task statements that share similar text, and ask Claude to describe these (Appendix C.1). Tasks not exposed to L L Ms or robots today tend to be hands-on and face-to-face, and are sometimes regulated.

111
00:27:50.111 --> 00:27:58.873
Healthcare tasks often combine these features, like “Administer medications to patients and monitor patients for reactions or side effects.”

112
00:27:59.323 --> 00:28:20.336
In Appendix C.2, we study which barriers may most prevent the automation of physical tasks. We ask Claude which barriers today would prevent robots from doing a significant share of each physical task, exposed or not, if unresolved. We group these into four categories: capabilities, human preferences, regulation, and cost.

113
00:28:20.456 --> 00:28:30.263
For tasks limited by capabilities, we have Claude pick the most important missing skill from manipulation, planning and reasoning, mobility and strength, and perception.

114
00:28:30.713 --> 00:28:49.913
We find that capabilities and costs are the biggest impediments to robot adoption. Capabilities prevent adoption for around 70 percent of physical tasks. Manipulation capabilities stand out: half of physical tasks wouldn’t be automated at scale unless robots become more adept at touching and handling objects.

115
00:28:50.033 --> 00:29:02.492
Shortcomings in planning and reasoning skills, which A I seems most likely to improve, limit robot automation for 8 percent of physical tasks. For nearly all tasks, costs would also need to fall.

116
00:29:02.942 --> 00:29:23.941
Our estimates indicate that today’s regulations wouldn’t allow robots to perform 14 percent of physical tasks. Regulation matters for tasks in healthcare, protective service, and education, but less so for those in food preparation, cleaning, production, construction, repair, and material moving, which make up about half of physical work.

117
00:29:24.061 --> 00:29:40.845
Human preferences hold back robots for a quarter of tasks. That may be because people wouldn’t trust a robot (to “Dress children and change diapers”) or because they value social interaction (to “Greet guests, escort them to their seats, and present them with menus and wine lists”).

118
00:29:41.295 --> 00:30:01.988
If robot capabilities develop quickly, what do these results imply? Around 30 percent of physical tasks are limited either by preferences or by regulation. That would leave most physical work exposed if robots became capable and cost-effective, though the rest may become weak links that drag down aggregate productivity speedups.

119
00:30:02.108 --> 00:30:22.225
Uneven progress in capabilities could also slow adoption, if robots became smarter but still couldn’t handle objects like humans. That said, better robots could also reduce resistance to automation, for example by assuaging safety concerns. We turn next to robot costs, the most common barrier in these estimates.

120
00:30:23.025 --> 00:30:24.661
Robot costs and adoption.

121
00:30:25.111 --> 00:30:48.806
Though robots can perform most physical work today, it may not be economical for them to do so. Robots made with pricey hardware aren’t as easily copied and distributed as software or L L Ms. Compared to L L M exposure measures that assess capabilities but not costs, it’s especially important to distinguish jobs exposed to cost-competitive robots.³⁴

122
00:30:49.256 --> 00:31:04.936
For every exposed task, we estimate the annual cost for robots to perform this task based on the specific robots cited in our task exposure measures. We prompt Claude to estimate costs using web search and the list of robots cited for each task.

123
00:31:05.056 --> 00:31:17.676
Automating an E1-exposed task could require redesigning workplaces to suit robots, while robots that do E3 tasks may use expensive sensors to navigate unstructured environments.

124
00:31:18.126 --> 00:31:40.031
Estimates cover the full cost of deploying a robot to perform each task.³⁵ To make robot and labor costs comparable, we first ask Claude to estimate how much a human worker typically produces on a task in a year. Bakers who “Roll, knead, cut, or shape dough,” for example, produce an estimated 180000 shaped pieces per year.

125
00:31:40.151 --> 00:31:59.690
Claude then estimates what it would cost a robot to produce the same output. Because bakers do this task in different environments, Claude averages robot deployment costs across retail, mid-scale, and industrial bakeries. Fixed costs are annualized to reflect hardware lifespans and the time value of money.

126
00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:04.901
Calculated this way, costs for robots to do a task can be compared to worker pay.

127
00:32:05.021 --> 00:32:24.345
We say a task is exposed to cost-competitive robots if its robot cost is less than its labor cost, calculated as the occupation’s total compensation from the BLS scaled by the fraction of time spent on this task.³⁶ Standard economic models predict that these tasks are automated,

128
00:32:24.465 --> 00:32:35.448
though given adoption frictions and cost uncertainties this measure is more suggestive.³⁷ See Appendix D.1 for details and a worked example of robot task costs.

129
00:32:35.898 --> 00:32:58.314
Robots often perform multiple job tasks, but we estimate costs for individual tasks in an occupation. Simply adding up task costs may double count robots or undercount coordination costs in real deployments. We aggregate by occupation, providing Claude with task cost estimates and asking for a total cost, net of redundancies and new additions.

130
00:32:58.434 --> 00:33:11.511
We say that an occupation is exposed to cost-competitive robots if this total robot cost is less than its total compensation scaled by the fraction of time spent on tasks that robots can do.³⁸

131
00:33:11.961 --> 00:33:25.044
Figure 7 shows five highly physical jobs and cost estimates for robots to perform their exposed tasks. Packers and packagers are the largest occupation exposed to cost-competitive robots.

132
00:33:25.164 --> 00:33:48.161
Several robots are needed to do packers and packagers’ tasks, including those that pack containers, move materials around warehouses, visually inspect goods, erect cardboard boxes, print and apply labels, and seal packages.³⁹ These robots are estimated to cost over 2 dollarsmillion to purchase and install, but replace the yearly work of around 14 workers.

133
00:33:48.281 --> 00:34:05.279
Fixed costs are spread over a roughly 10-year service life at an 8 percent cost of capital. Operating expenses, such as maintenance, part-time human supervision, and energy, bring annual costs to around 45000 dollarsto replace one human worker per year.

134
00:34:05.399 --> 00:34:19.541
Since packers and packagers spend 97 percent of their time on tasks that robots can do and cost around 49000, dollarsrobots cost about 2500 dollarsless per year to do that work.

135
00:34:19.991 --> 00:34:46.011
Figure 7: Figure 7: Robot costs for physical occupations Employment and total compensation use data from the BLS (OEWS 2025, ECEC 2026). Exposed tasks are the share of that occupation’s tasks at exposure E1 to E3, weighted by estimated time spent on each task. Claude estimates annual costs for robots to perform exposed tasks for each occupation.

136
00:34:46.131 --> 00:34:56.754
Costs include annualized fixed costs and variable costs. The final column lists one robot included in each cost estimate and its estimated purchase price.

137
00:34:57.204 --> 00:35:09.189
The U S currently employs around 560000 packers and packagers. Firms face adoption frictions, like regulation or borrowing limits, and these cost estimates are approximate.

138
00:35:09.309 --> 00:35:30.668
For example, robotaxis are estimated to cost only around 7000 dollarsmore than taxi drivers, but face regulatory hurdles.⁴⁰ That said, automation risks are likely higher for physical jobs that cost-competitive robots can do. Employment for packers and packagers has fallen 22 percent since 2015.

139
00:35:30.788 --> 00:35:43.673
And the BLS projects this occupation will shed the 11th-most jobs of any occupation by 2035, behind only correctional officers and 9 sales and office occupations.⁴¹

140
00:35:44.123 --> 00:36:07.547
For the other jobs shown in Figure 7, robots cost much more than humans even though they can do much of the work. In metalworking, robots with cameras and A I can weld autonomously.⁴² But human welders perform many tasks that enable welding, like positioning large metal parts, climbing ladders to weld hard-to-reach joints, checking quality, and grinding and finishing materials.

141
00:36:07.667 --> 00:36:13.640
Automating this work requires robots that together cost around five times more than human welders.

142
00:36:14.090 --> 00:36:34.503
Dishwashers, along with janitors and cleaners, are paid 25000 dollarsto 30000 dollarsless than welders. But their robot analogs are still several times more expensive. Cleaning is hard to standardize and streamline, and robot cleaners are often slower than humans and limited to specific tasks.⁴³

143
00:36:34.953 --> 00:36:52.852
Figure 8 summarizes robot costs by plotting exposure to cost-competitive robots at hypothetical, uniform declines in robot costs. For example, if robots cost 20 percent less today, they would be cost-competitive for the physical work done by 2.8 million workers.

144
00:36:52.972 --> 00:37:16.111
These workers spend on average 42 percent of their time on work exposed to robots, 0.8 percent of all working time in the economy. For reference, industry and government data suggest that robot prices have declined roughly 3 percent per year since the 1990s. At this rate, it would take around seven years to reach a 20 percent cost decline.

145
00:37:16.231 --> 00:37:20.705
Appendix E discusses historical robot price data in more detail.

146
00:37:21.155 --> 00:37:30.246
Though robots can theoretically do tasks that add up to 34 percent of all work time, they are cost-competitive for just 0.3 percent today.

147
00:37:30.366 --> 00:37:47.869
Still, that includes roughly 300000 workers for whom robots can do 95 percent of their tasks.⁴⁴ As Figure 7 suggested, robots today are cost-competitive or close to it for packers and packagers and taxi drivers, but far from it for most other jobs.

148
00:37:48.319 --> 00:38:09.139
For robots to be cost-competitive for 10 percent of human work today, costs would need to decline about 70 percent. At a 3 percent decline per year, that would take around 40 years. That said, it’s possible that new, more capable robots like humanoids or new manufacturing processes will drive down costs to automate human work.

149
00:38:09.259 --> 00:38:16.442
Reports suggest that global production could scale quickly if there were intense demand for humanoids and other robots.⁴⁵

150
00:38:16.892 --> 00:38:39.838
Figure 8: Figure 8: Work exposed under hypothetical robot cost declines Calculated by comparing robot and labor costs. Labor costs are the fraction of time spent on exposed tasks multiplied by occupation total compensation. Given robot cost declines, the curve plots the share of all job tasks for which robots cost less than labor.

151
00:38:39.958 --> 00:39:02.625
Tasks are weighted by estimated time demands and occupation employment. Robot and labor costs are compared at six points in the worker pay distribution; see Appendix D.2 for details. Selected occupations are marked where their robot costs fall below their median labor cost. For example, robots do 94 percent of Postal Service mail carrier tasks.

152
00:39:02.745 --> 00:39:34.159
To match what workers produce in a year would cost 166000 dollarswith robots. Median total compensation for Postal Service mail carriers is 82000, dollarsso robots would be cost-competitive if cost declined 54 percent (166000 dollarsx 46 percent< 82000 dollarsx 94 percent). Total compensation is calculated with data from the BLS (OEWS 2025, ECEC 2026).

153
00:39:34.609 --> 00:39:52.033
Cost parity between humans and robots also needn’t imply high unemployment or rapid economic growth. Tasks that workers still perform may become bottlenecks, and these robot cost estimates often include human supervisory, exception-handling, or repair work.

154
00:39:52.153 --> 00:40:06.654
As emphasized by Acemoglu and Restrepo (2019) and Jones and Tonetti (2026), automation boosts productivity only after machines are much cheaper than humans, rather than at cost parity.

155
00:40:07.104 --> 00:40:29.355
We apply these cost estimates in two extensions. Appendix D.2 calculates an alternative occupation exposure measure that factors in both what robots can do and how cheaply they can do it. Accounting for cost produces a very similar ranking of jobs compared to our main, capabilities-focused measure (0.95 rank correlation).

156
00:40:29.805 --> 00:40:47.853
Appendix E also presents stylized scenarios for robot adoption if robots become cheaper and more productive. The scenarios draw on episodes of innovation and adoption for industrial robots since the 1990s, as well as analyst forecasts for humanoids.

157
00:40:47.973 --> 00:40:57.061
We hold fixed tasks and wages and ask how soon historical rates of cost declines and capability gains would make robots cost-competitive.

158
00:40:57.511 --> 00:41:19.671
To model how robots take on new work over time, we rate exposure for 1977 job tasks using today’s robots, and compare with robot exposure as of 1977. In 1977, robots could not do 62 percent of physical tasks. Today’s robots can do all but 24 percent of those same tasks.

159
00:41:19.791 --> 00:41:27.683
Over time, that means each year robots became able to do 2 percent of the tasks they previously could not (see Appendix B.4).

160
00:41:28.133 --> 00:41:40.783
Adding in 3 percent cost declines per year, robots aren’t cost-competitive for half of physical work today until 2085. But rapid advances in robotics may speed up these timelines.

161
00:41:40.903 --> 00:42:00.125
In a fast adoption scenario, where quality-adjusted costs fall up to four times faster and robots become able to do new tasks twice as fast, robots become cost-competitive for half of physical work by 2050. Automating 90 percent of physical work today still takes 53 years.

162
00:42:00.575 --> 00:42:21.055
One caveat is that our scenarios apply the same cost decline or capability increase to every task, so tasks become cost-competitive at today’s ranking of costs relative to worker pay. That ranking captures broad economic incentives, but robotics companies may also target certain capabilities first for other reasons.

163
00:42:21.175 --> 00:42:34.721
Humanoid demonstrations sometimes showcase household chores, for example, while robot makers may prioritize factory work useful to their own operations, much as A I companies prioritized coding agents.⁴⁶

164
00:42:35.171 --> 00:42:56.182
Overall, robots would need to sustain record rates of price declines and quality improvements over the coming decades to enable rapid physical automation. Even so, job impacts are less certain with these cost projections, since they set aside forces like preferences and regulation as well as feedback effects like falling wages.

165
00:42:56.982 --> 00:42:57.769
Discussion.

166
00:42:58.219 --> 00:43:13.598
We introduce a new measure of job exposure to robots. A job is exposed when robots can perform its tasks. Robots that work like humans count more toward exposure than those that require rebuilding the workplace, like factory robots.

167
00:43:14.048 --> 00:43:37.424
We find that driving jobs are most exposed, due to autonomous vehicles. Most robots today aren’t like driverless cars, and only operate in controlled environments. But robots could perform most physical work in some capacity, though they are several times more expensive than humans for the same jobs. Jobs exposed to robots are quite different from those exposed to L L Ms.

168
00:43:37.544 --> 00:43:40.057
They pay less, and are more physically demanding.

169
00:43:40.507 --> 00:43:56.913
We hope these measures help analyze physical automation as A I expands what robots can do. Exposure patterns today may inform which jobs more capable robots will disrupt tomorrow. And periodic updates to this work can track robot capabilities.

170
00:43:57.363 --> 00:44:20.105
Our exposure scale is predicated on the idea that jobs are more at risk when today’s robots already do them in some settings. We find that this way of assessing robots predicts historical labor market impacts. But A I-powered robots could leapfrog our scale and do work they cannot today, for example by learning to climb ladders or use their arms and grippers more deftly.

171
00:44:20.555 --> 00:44:43.288
Much remains uncertain about how robots and A I could reshape the economy. Growth forecasts for A I often assume that physical bottlenecks, which robots could loosen, will drag down gains from automating cognitive work. We also have not considered how A I could affect physical work without robots, for example by better predicting when factory machines need maintenance.

172
00:44:43.408 --> 00:45:01.775
In the nearer term, efforts to track A I’s impacts could watch exposed occupations, like drivers and warehouse packers, for early signs of disruption. Further out, a key question is how work itself will change, perhaps becoming more social and interpersonal as robots and A I advance.

