375
01:33:49.853 --> 01:34:11.666
The International Organization for Standardization defines a robot as a “programmed actuated mechanism with a degree of autonomy to perform locomotion, manipulation or positioning” and autonomy as the “ability to perform intended tasks based on current state and sensing, without human intervention” (ISO 2021).

376
01:34:11.906 --> 01:34:31.126
11. Our focus on what today’s robots can do is similar to Eloundou and others (2024), who measure A I exposure by what tasks L L Ms could speed up as of 2023. Other A I exposure measures are described in Massenkoff and McCrory (2026).

377
01:34:31.366 --> 01:34:56.554
12. See Autor (2015) on “environmental control” as a bottleneck to automation, and Autor and others (2020) on how integration slows robot adoption. A report on the economic impacts of robotics for the National Institute of Standards and Technology highlights robot capabilities in unstructured environments; see Section 6 of Link and others (2016).

378
01:34:56.674 --> 01:35:27.140
Kober and others (2013), Ibarz and others (2021), and Kim and others (2025) discuss how robot performance generalizes to new tasks and environments. See Firoozi and others (2025) on robotic foundation models versus specialized robot control systems. For surveys on progress in robotic manipulation, see Kemp and others (2007) and Kroemer and others (2021).

379
01:35:27.260 --> 01:35:39.847
See Pumacay and others (2024) for manipulation benchmarks. For an inventory of robot capabilities in complex environments, see Rivière and Denain (2026).

380
01:35:40.087 --> 01:35:50.044
